  "body": "Sites can use the [Content-Security-Policy](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CSP) header in order to protect their content from cross-site attacks (e.g. XSS, CSRF). One of the directives you can use this header for is to [sandbox](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/sandbox) contexts that render media URLs and the browser will treat that document as an opaque origin. In Chrome, when blocking third-party cookies, we noticed this leads to some unexpected user-facing breakage when sending the header on top-level loads to video or image resources.\r\n\r\nTo reproduce it:\r\n\r\n1. Navigate to https://cr2.kungfoo.net/cookies/v2/\r\n\r\n2. Click \"Set cookie (SameSite=None)\"\r\n\r\n3. Navigate to https://cr2.kungfoo.net/cookies/v2/video.php and the video should load.\r\n\r\n3. In another tab, open chrome://settings/cookies and turn on 3P cookie blocking.\r\n\r\n4. Refresh the other tab, the video should no longer be able to load because the cookie is no longer sent in the data request.\r\n\r\nBrowser engines render a sandboxed top-level document, and then some will make a second request for the media for a `<video>` or `<img>` tag embedded in the document.\r\n\r\nAs browsers move towards removing third-party cookies, browsers that use [site for cookies](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-httpbis-rfc6265bis-12#section-5.2) to block third-party cookies (like Chrome) will block any cookies in the second request. This is because the site for cookies is always cross-site when a request originates from an opaque origin. If the media content is access-controlled using a cookie, this will break users loading the content in their browser. The resource will load without issues in Firefox and Safari.\r\n\r\nTo remedy this, we propose standardizing an exception to third-party cookie blocking when we are rendering media URLs that use the CSP sandbox directive only on the request for the media. We believe this will provide a carve-out to preserve functionality in a way that minimizes the risk to privacy and security.\r\n\r\nWould appreciate thoughts from @annevk @freddyb and others.",
